findings.body:
Lines 25-27 unconditionally overwrite a missing refresh_token with the literal text "refresh_token":

```ts
if (!refreshTokenResponse.data.refresh_token) {
  refreshTokenResponse.data.refresh_token = "refresh_token";
}
```

The intent appears to be to preserve the original refresh_token from the database (since self-hosters refresh tokens on the parent app), but the code instead stores the literal string "refresh_token". After the first refresh under APP_CREDENTIAL_SHARING_ENABLED, the stored credential has an invalid refresh_token, so every subsequent token refresh that uses `credentialKey.refresh_token` (e.g. SalesForce `CalendarService.ts` line 81) will fail or attempt to refresh with a bogus token. For apps that go through `parseRefreshTokenResponse` (googlecalendar, office365calendar, salesforce, zoomvideo, webex) this permanently breaks the OAuth flow.

Lines 5-11 build the schema as:

```ts
const minimumTokenResponseSchema = z.object({
  access_token: z.string(),
  [z.string().toString()]: z.number(),
  [z.string().optional().toString()]: z.unknown().optional(),
});
```

`z.object()` does not support dynamic/computed keys this way. The expressions evaluate to the literal strings "ZodString" and "ZodOptional", which become static keys on the schema. The schema therefore requires the response to literally have keys named "ZodString" and "ZodOptional" - no real OAuth token response has those keys, so `safeParse` always fails. The author clearly meant `z.record(z.string(), z.number())` to allow arbitrary numeric expiry fields. As written, the schema either fails to construct or always rejects real responses, breaking every integration that calls this function when credential sharing is enabled.
